A||||8||Johnson's85SE||Ticking noise, need advise please||||13||
Z||000000||Johnson's85SE||01-18-2001||01:01 PM||||Hello, <BR>  Just need some advise please. My car has a ticking noise in the engine compartment, that amplifies when the throttle is let wide open. The noise quites down when the engine is warm, but is still there. I would like to know if this is a cracked exhast manifold(firewall) or if my second engine is starting to go? The sound is like a sowing machine. Any information about what engine knock, and a exhast manifold leaks, sound like would be greatly appreciated. It is an 1985 V6, 4SPd with 200KMs on the body. And around 150KMS on the engine in the car. I look after my car, 5K oil changes, dont pop the cluch, tune ups, 92 Octane.  Thanks, Chris <BR>||||reg||13
Z||000001||Cheever2||01-18-2001||01:53 PM||||My 88 4-cyl had that even AFTER a tuneup.  Turned out to be a bad plug wire.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000002||mblackwell||01-18-2001||02:04 PM||||My 88 Formula start4ed making that sound recently, I hope its ok.||||reg||1
Z||000003||LAMBO||01-18-2001||02:14 PM||||My guess would be lifters. With those kind of miles, they could be going bad (gunked up and not properly "pumping up"). It could be a cracked manifold, but a loudness of ticking change after warm up doesn't jive with that. You can easily check for this by using a long piece of vaccum tubing and placing one end in your ear while probing around the manifolds with the other end. If there is a leak, it will be amplified through the tubing, kinda like a doctor's stethiscope. <BR>Hope this helps, <BR>Todd||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||01-18-2001||02:21 PM||||My cracked manifold got quieter after warm up.  I think when the manifold expands it closes the size of the crack down some.<P>The stethescope is a good idea. For internal engine noises you can also use something like a broom handle and touch it to different areas while holding the other end sort of cupped to your ear.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||01-18-2001||02:24 PM||||Check ALL your ignition componets which deliver spark to the plugs, replace as necessary.<BR>Then use a screw driver like a stethoscope, or go get one at the auto parts store and isolate the side that you 'tick' is comming from and adjust your valves.<BR>You might want to try at your next oil change (since you use a longer interval than normal) to get some motor flush and use it before you change it, might just be some sludge blocking a lifter.<P>OOOOHHHH.......DHR is quick too!<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 01-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||01-18-2001||02:46 PM||||I'm with DRH on this one.  Normal clicking noises on the V6 come from the injectors and the valve train.  If it is not the ordinary injector clicking it is likely an exhaust leak.  And yes, they can close up after the engine warms up.||||reg||1
Z||000007||My7Fieros||01-18-2001||03:10 PM||||Forward exhaust manifold....have fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://tgowens.com/My7Fieros_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Johnson's85SE||01-18-2001||06:15 PM||||Thanks, to all that have replied. Can wait till spring to start taking things apart.<BR>Chris||||reg||1
